event: response.completed
data: {"type":"response.completed","sequence_number":272,"safety_buffering":false,"response":{"id":"resp_0830161e0e5a82b9016ac91ef7250481938dbeb18a10bba4f6","object":"response","created_at":1791565559,"completed_at":1791565574,"status":"completed","background":false,"frequency_penalty":0.0,"presence_penalty":0.0,"moderation":null,"model":"gpt-6.1-sol","access_programs":{"cyber":"standard"},"previous_response_id":null,"instructions":"You are an expert coding assistant operating inside pi, a coding agent harness. You help users by reading files, executing commands, editing code, and writing new files.\n\n<tools>\n- read: Read file contents\n- bash: Execute bash commands (ls, grep, find, etc.)\n- edit: Make precise file edits with exact text replacement, including multiple disjoint edits in one call\n- write: Create or overwrite files\n\nIn addition to the tools above, you may have access to other custom tools depending on the project.\n</tools>\n\n<rules>\n- Use bash for file operations like ls, rg, find\n- Use read to examine files instead of cat or sed.\n- You can inspect PI_* environment variables for current model and session details.\n- Use edit for precise changes (edits[].oldText must match exactly)\n- When changing multiple separate locations in one file, use one edit call with multiple entries in edits[] instead of multiple edit calls\n- Each edits[].oldText is matched against the original file, not after earlier edits are applied. Do not emit overlapping or nested edits. Merge nearby changes into one edit.\n- Keep edits[].oldText as small as possible while still being unique in the file. Do not pad with large unchanged regions.\n- Use write only for new files or complete rewrites.\n- Be concise in your responses\n- Show file paths clearly when working with files\n</rules>\n\n<docs>\nPi documentation (read only when the user asks about pi itself, its SDK, extensions, themes, skills, or TUI):\n- Main documentation: /usr/local/share/npm-global/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/README.md\n- Additional docs: /usr/local/share/npm-global/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/docs\n- Examples: /usr/local/share/npm-global/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/examples (extensions, custom tools, SDK)\n- When reading pi docs or examples, resolve docs/... under Additional docs and examples/... under Examples, not the current working directory\n- When asked about: extensions (docs/extensions.md, examples/extensions/), themes (docs/themes.md), skills (docs/skills.md), prompt templates (docs/prompt-templates.md), TUI components (docs/tui.md), keybindings (docs/keybindings.md), SDK integrations (docs/sdk.md), custom providers (docs/custom-provider.md), adding models (docs/models.md), pi packages (docs/packages.md), environment variables (docs/environment-variables.md), MCP servers (docs/mcp.md), codemode scripts and non-LLM models such as classifiers and image models (docs/codemode.md)\n- When working on pi topics, read the docs and examples, and follow .md cross-references before implementing\n- Always read pi .md files completely and follow links to related docs (e.g., tui.md for TUI API details)\n</docs>\n\n<addendum>\nKeep changes focused and readable. Use clear names and avoid needless duplication. Verify the changed behavior with appropriate checks before submitting.\n</addendum>\n\n<cwd>\n/home/agent/work\n</cwd>","output":[{"type":"reasoning","id":"rs_0830161e0e5a82b9016ac91ef87af08193a30a6eec29ed8c38","summary":[{"type":"summary_text","text":"**Considering race condition fix**\n\nI need to address a race condition that occurs when reloading after unchecking a checkbox. The best approach is to use a synchronous XMLHttpRequest, but that's deprecated and not ideal. An alternative is to use an async fetch to ensure the checkbox remains checked until navigation. However, I think employing normal submission might be effective. Playwright requires the checkbox to be unchecked immediately, but it's a tricky situation given that synchronous XHR is often more reliable."},{"type":"summary_text","text":"**Exploring request handling strategies**\n\nI could use fetch with keepalive, which ensures the request isn't canceled during a reload, but I'm worried that a GET request might overtake a POST. The server parsing could lead to timing issues, creating a race condition. Using synchronous XHR is still a good option for immediate reloads. It allows explicit saves, but I wonder if async fetch would work better. Ultimately, I think going with synchronous XHR might be the best choice despite its drawbacks for reliable updates. I'll need to ensure everything is tested properly!"}],"content":[],"encrypted_content":"gAAAAABqyR8FMp699GfI-O1Hq3shGZ4zCVQb6MB-PxTpqD0IXhWKlzdxXkvI9jywUum7SopedV-J9NFoCkGQ2zHmAlAhIz5SsrHYl1QCI0G2RJSEtzfHnZiaoc07F91B11V9W8eyQqt1JIdBKYQnI-NzmF7bMpuW6rFxQobwxWRDVSV5hCSsDrzbs8igIOb4DIGnUqDNO-tlZ_WBLGVCi9xq6QRzy96icLmfIo6Phlv8HJo8MYiZFp4iE9P3Z5OfCksJtC1WkoJq5HRRbPmUe8wus5AfZxhrYJXI2G2GFdhN-xLhgsUuyl2KdxuE4fBdJ_9z1DVT6sDmbcdhqLujxhRNEHsyiqRHf5mHqtOmXkvycqQG3kYKskxo07dNbpq5n2HeXkjOfW-x9y_exxIsppd7RqdYMb7Nc3rLHbmcdy2nVLcqXeIow4TQbsWmS8HfspKe0DZe74rVHudc-4qui8v8psBBhWh_CYg03PtGjjzHspn0T8y8XcmFGHfYE-fL6P_g-Z3HM3Xq_PGO1p2kqyYzyLzDpx13YJh6jxumd5h8O5LYJhXA5nUFAHO2tGokRvFiUO-OSxgJlC4m1LWAYU8AUZzXI_Pnh8ZfsvGDtr5in_rjpUbNgf8Oh3mHO4PwXd0lWnuUib7YQnXYXHyiO2HXyuuCb8XYM4-XA-84hljQMlJ23a7QWj-GDFHqVoLraGkKjJP5AgVf00xSytjXrCm1G70g_zKi4x2g8G7r94tDVaw9zoXXn7jKpECrN-e5HwYuK9XGdyfhYITN_LnSN78Ww5YXUkKjDr1SsHiGlB1zzsBvL_u1U2Sw3c4JGAyzx85ph30hNll2jpW8_Ymj-6Qwa2EXrNv_XuQ_bL74W0Zomc0jiLOgVjyVizvyDK89h3b6f8gnttioUzD-KPSvDjo4mj4YJqK0I5gKDf3if28OE2T0BRVUHiIllAvlwwCItvOckSXgm87GzkB7cTKKgouke-K0_LWHkptwAI37EUfGDmnOOEOEuMt-QWsbchR0CX3FKzKkicnHA4yNo4IllNAPlQ2ruje9ojMc7GuH5ynpEGiamEjWQYAsydEvsL0zcTF4NHMWoEik2y5VaXxxBDKCYp7LKjy-9SjkBG8p16qiNaQLfxnqAW2gCVkRFr0HVkDsx4P_y20LRs9s-sX5QRo6pRIm-47u-kx7_Zpowja5t31P7OxZGqrjxlbUCA_-umX4574llovoyJxYv5V6F_69LhW-8sELAlxzB0ZWBIUGzIm2EN5hwCSZc643s26m